# A scanner's answer is a two-field record that travels in two registers:
# the position packed above the value's tag in one word, the value's payload
# in the other. A consumer whose arm destructures the record reads the words
# directly and no record is ever built. An arm that names the whole value,
# like `r@(parsed p v)`, wants the record itself, so a slot with such an arm
# is passed boxed for every arm, and the scanner builds a record per answer.
#
# `e@(err _)` is not that arm. The dispatcher reads the two words back as one
# value before it matches, and on the failure path that value is the failure
# that arrived, so the name binds to exactly what the caller sent and nothing
# has to be built. The 2026-09-15 ruling asks every consumer of a fallible
# scanner for an arm like this one, and until 2026-09-16 the arm turned the
# two-register convention off: the json decoder built 253,968,000 bytes of
# records it had built none of the day before.
#
# Every group in this loop hands back a `parsed`, so the loop is not a beat
# and nothing crosses a carry; the only record this program could build is
# the one the as-pattern would ask for. `sh_rec` is the pin: 0 with the arm
# read as the failure case it is, 64,000 with it read as an ordinary
# as-pattern, a record for each of the thousand scans.
type parsed
  pos
  val

fn scan 0
  err "end of input"

fn scan n
  parsed n (n * 2)

fn step (parsed p v) acc
  walk (p - 1) (acc + v)

fn step e@(err _) acc
  parsed acc (length e.reason)

fn walk n acc
  step (scan n) acc

fn report (parsed total reason_length)
  "total {total}, stopped by a reason {reason_length} characters long"

pub play = print (report (walk 1000 0))
